          <p>As school kicks off and my schedule ramps up, I finally have the chance to share some exciting news: I
            passed the CompTIA A+ 1101 exam! With weeks of studying behind me, followed by a well-deserved break to
            recharge and gear up for the new school year, it’s good to be back on the blog.</p>

          <h3 class="wp-block-heading">My Study Tools and Techniques</h3>
          <p>For my 1101 exam prep, I relied heavily on the <strong>Total Seminars course on Udemy</strong>. Given my
            background in building, using, and repairing personal computers, I found myself already familiar with many
            of the exam objectives. The hands-on tech projects I’ve tackled over the past few months, like tweaking my
            router settings, setting up Pihole, and managing my server, proved invaluable.</p>
          <p>After completing the course, I consistently scored around 70% on practice tests. To close the gap, I dived
            into Mike Meyers’ book, taking detailed notes on areas I struggled with, especially printers. I also want to
            give a shoutout to <strong>mirandawallace on Reddit</strong> for sharing an <a
              href="https://quizlet.com/user/mirandawallace/folders">incredibly helpful folder</a> of A+ terms on
            Quizlet. It made a world of difference.</p>
          <p>In addition to the coursework, I took on a couple of hands-on projects to boost my confidence:
            disassembling and reassembling an old laptop and replacing the screen on a Nexus 5 I rooted to install Kali
            Nethunter. While these tasks weren’t strictly necessary, they solidified my skills and gave me the extra
            assurance I needed before taking the exam.</p>

          <h3 class="wp-block-heading">What’s Next: Preparing for the 1102 Exam</h3>
          <p>This week, I officially began studying for the 1102 exam. A quick glance at the objectives shows that I
            have less familiarity with this material compared to 1101. Although I have tried my hand at each of the
            topics included in the objectives, they're the kind of things I would Google as they came up rather than
            know by rote. I skipped over a lot of the 1102-specific sections in Mike Meyers’ book, so I’ll need to go
            back and give those a thorough read.</p>
          <p>To broaden my experience, I borrowed a MacBook from my sister-in-law, since my exposure to macOS is pretty
            limited. My goal is to be exam-ready by mid-October, during Fall Break. While the 1102 exam might demand
            more practice and memorization, I feel confident in the study groove I’ve developed. With nine weeks to go,
            I’m optimistic that I’ll be well-prepared.</p>
